### Description

For `service on new L("one"), new L("two") { ... }`, nutcracker evaluates every listener expression first, then constructs the service object, then attaches to each listener. Observed order: `listener init one`, `listener init two`, `service init`, `attach one`, `attach two`.

Spec §8.3.2 ([Service declaration](https://ballerina.io/spec/lang/master/#section_8.3.2)): "A service-decl is initialized as part of module initialization as follows. The object-constructor-block is evaluated as in an object-constructor-expr resulting in a service object s. Then for each expression in expression-list: the expression is evaluated resulting in a value which is either an error or an object obj belonging to a listener object type; if it is an error, module initialization fails; otherwise, obj is registered as a module listener ...; s is then attached to obj using obj's attach method; if the call to attach fails, then module initialization fails."

So the expected order is `service init`, `listener init one`, `attach one`, `listener init two`, `attach two`; and when the first `attach` fails, module initialization fails before `new L("two")` is evaluated. The reproducer below shows the observable side effect: the second listener is constructed even though attaching to the first already failed.

Expected output: `listener init one`, then panic `attach failed one`.
Actual output: `listener init one`, `listener init two`, then `error: attach failed one`.

The cause appears to be the desugaring in `desugar/desugar.go` (`hoistInlineServiceListeners` hoists inline listener expressions into module-level listener variables that are initialized before `buildServiceInitStmts` runs). If this ordering was chosen deliberately to match jBallerina, it still deviates from the spec text above.

### Steps to Reproduce

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

class L {
    string n;

    function init(string n) {
        self.n = n;
        io:println("listener init ", n); // @output listener init one
    }

    public function attach(service object {} svc, () attachPoint = ()) returns error? {
        var _ = svc;
        var _ = attachPoint;
        return error("attach failed " + self.n);
    }

    public function detach(service object {} svc) returns error? {
        var _ = svc;
    }

    public function 'start() returns error? {
    }

    public function gracefulStop() returns error? {
    }

    public function immediateStop() returns error? {
    }
}

service on new L("one"), new L("two") { // @panic attach failed one
}

public function main() {
}
```

### Affected Version(s)

`main` @ `00d6965be`

